package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyPlaneswalkers = StorySection(
    "planeswalkers", "Planeswalkers",
    "For most of the Multiverse's history a planeswalker was something close to a god, and the story since has been the slow taking away of that power, first by the Mending and then by the end of the Phyrexian invasion.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "oldwalkers", "Oldwalkers",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "will-made-flesh", "Will Made Flesh",
                    listOf(
                        "A planeswalker is a being with a spark, a mark of the Blind Eternities on the soul. Perhaps one in several million is born with one, and almost none of those ever find out. The spark sleeps until something tears it open, a brush with death or a grief too large to carry, and then the bearer steps out of their world and into the chaos between worlds. Many do not survive the first step. They come back mad, or they do not come back at all.",
                        "Those who did survive, in the old days, became something that mortals had no word for. The planeswalkers of the ancient Multiverse did not age. Their bodies were not flesh so much as a shape their will chose to wear, and they could change it as easily as a cloak. Given time and effort they could build whole planes of their own and people them. They carried armies with them between worlds. Mortals worshipped them, and the planeswalkers, living for thousands of years among creatures who lived for sixty, very often stopped seeing those mortals as anything more than weather.",
                        "In the earliest stories any wizard strong enough could become a planeswalker, and the later canon replaced that with the spark, which a person either has or does not. The most famous ignition of the old kind came at the end of the Brothers' War. When [Urza](who/urza) set off the {Golgothian Sylex} at [Argoth](sylex-blast), the blast that ended his war and his brother also woke his spark, and the two halves of the old powerstone became his eyes. He walked out of a dead continent as something close to immortal.",
                        "That was what a planeswalker was, for most of the history of the Multiverse. A god who happened to be a person, or a person who had stopped being one. The gods had their own quarrels, and the worlds they walked through paid for them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "serras-realm", "Serra's Realm",
                    listOf(
                        "Serra came to Dominaria thousands of years ago and made the first angels there. In the old Sheoltun Empire, forerunner of Benalia, she was revered as a goddess, and a temple with a winged statue of her stood at Epityr long after anyone remembered why. Then she did what only the oldwalkers could do. Out of white mana she built a plane of her own, Serra's Realm, a heaven of light and wings, and filled it with angels and with human worshippers who wrote down her teachings as the Song of All.",
                        "It was ruined by a guest. [Urza](who/urza) arrived there wounded by Phyrexians and spent years healing in Serra's care, and when he left in peace, the Phyrexians who had been tracking him came through after him. They killed many of her people, and the black mana of their presence soaked into the Realm so deeply that Serra could no longer bear to live in it. She left with those who would follow, and put the archangel Radiant in charge of what remained.",
                        "On the plane of Ulgrotha she met another planeswalker, Feroz, who thought summoning living things to fight was a kind of slavery. They married. Together they stood with the people of Ulgrotha against the vampire Baron Sengir, and when the planeswalker Taysir chased one of Feroz's students home, Feroz beat him and then sealed the whole plane off from outsiders with a great ward, {Feroz's Ban}. It protected Ulgrotha. It did not protect Feroz, who died in an accident in his own laboratory, studying a fire elemental trapped in ice.",
                        "Serra fled to Sursi on Dominaria and hid among wandering refugees. When a planeswalker disguised as a thief attacked her, she could have healed the wound easily. She did not. She let herself fade, and spent her last strength blessing the ground around her, and the man who had tried to save her built a cathedral over her tomb. {Serra the Benevolent} is the goddess as she was before any of it. Her Realm, left to the proud Radiant, slowly turned Phyrexian, until Urza came back aboard the [Weatherlight](the-legacy) and collapsed the dying plane into the ship's powerstone.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Feroz is gone, but what remains is his final gift to his people: the right to be left alone.", "Feroz's Ban"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "gods-of-the-ice", "Gods of the Ice",
                    listOf(
                        "The Sylex Blast that made [Urza](who/urza) a planeswalker also froze Dominaria, and the long winter that followed was crowded with gods. One of the newest was Freyalise. She grew up in the city of Storgard as a fire mage, and when a manipulated king ordered her to duel her childhood friend Jason Carthalion, his green magic struck her down and her spark ignited with that power in her mind. The ascension nearly drove her mad. A mortal archmage named Jodah talked her back.",
                        "She became the patron goddess of the elves of Fyndhorn and Llanowar, keeping them alive through the [Ice Age](ice-age), and in the end she was one of those who cast the World Spell that broke the winter. Others of her kind were less kind. Tevesh Szat had been the hidden influence on the king who ordered her duel. Leshrac, who walked in the night and was proud of it, plotted with Szat to conquer the mana-rich plane of Shandalar using an army of the dead raised for them on Dominaria by the necromancer Lim-Dul. When a planeswalker named Faralyn called the others to a summit, the gathering turned violent, because Faralyn had wanted a planeswalker's death to power his own escape.",
                        "Leshrac got to Shandalar and lost there. He later ruled the Kush region of Dominaria until the planeswalker Taysir hunted him down and locked him in a prison on Phyrexia, where he stayed for ages. Lord Windgrace, a panther warrior older than Urza, remembered Urborg as a forest before the blast drowned it, and hated every machine he ever saw because of what one machine had done to his home. {Freyalise, Llanowar's Fury} shows the goddess in her armour, with the eyepatch she wore in later centuries.",
                        "These were the beings Urza needed when he finally went to war with Phyrexia. Gods were the only weapon he had left that was big enough, and he set about recruiting them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "nine-titans", "Nine Titans",
                    listOf(
                        "For his last campaign [Urza](who/urza) gathered nine planeswalkers, himself among them, and called them the Titans. Freyalise came, and Lord Windgrace. So did the sorceress Daria and her stepfather Taysir, Kristina of the Woods, the wanderer Bo Levar, the eccentric Commodore Guff, and Tevesh Szat. [Teferi](who/teferi) had been asked first, and refused. Rather than join, he spent the opening of the [Invasion](invasion) phasing great pieces of Dominaria out of time altogether, to keep them from the Phyrexians, which left holes in the world that would matter later.",
                        "Urza built each Titan a suit of armour, a walking war machine with falcon engines in its chest to hunt glistening oil and rockets at its wrists, and in them they carried the war to Phyrexia itself. There Tevesh Szat betrayed them, as Urza had known he would. Szat killed Daria and Kristina before Urza turned on him and used his life to power the soul bombs the Titans had come to plant. {Urza Assembles the Titans} tells the gathering.",
                        "Urza's failure followed. Standing in the heart of the plane he had hated for four thousand years, he could not bring himself to destroy it. He dismantled the master bomb that would have set off all the others, and surrendered himself to [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth). Freyalise, Guff, Bo Levar and Windgrace set off the bombs they had planted anyway and came home to find Yawgmoth already walking on Dominaria. Only the firing of the Legacy weapon at the end of the [Apocalypse](apocalypse) saved the world, and of the nine Titans only Freyalise and Windgrace lived through the war.",
                        "It was the last great act of the old gods, and it left Dominaria scarred in ways no one saw yet. Every one of those godlike spells, the Sylex, Urza's experiments with time, Teferi's vanished lands, had cracked something underneath reality. Three centuries later the cracks opened, and the gods who were left found themselves paying the bill in the [Time Rifts](time-rifts).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Antiquities, Homelands, Ice Age, Invasion, Apocalypse"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Gathering Dark, The Eternal Ice and The Shattered Alliance, by Jeff Grubb"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Invasion, Planeshift and Apocalypse, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Story of Homelands, The Duelist magazine"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Planeswalker/Lore, Serra, Feroz, Freyalise, Leshrac, Windgrace, Nine Titans"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "time-rifts", "Time Rifts",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "a-starving-world", "A Starving World",
                    listOf(
                        "Three hundred years after the Phyrexians were beaten, Dominaria began to starve. Mana drained out of the land the way water drains from a cracked cistern. Forests withered into toxic scrub, rivers sank, and the survivors of one apocalypse found themselves living through a slower one. Above the worst of the ruin hung rifts, wounds in the sky where time itself had torn.",
                        "Each rift had been made by a catastrophe, and nearly every catastrophe had a planeswalker behind it. The oldest was at Madara, where [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had fought a demonic leviathan to the death thousands of years before and broken a third of the continent. The [Sylex Blast](sylex-blast) had torn one over Yavimaya. [Urza](who/urza)'s failed time machine had torn one over Tolaria. When [Teferi](who/teferi) phased Shiv and Zhalfir out of the world during the [Invasion](invasion), he left two more. The overlay of Rath had dropped Volrath's Stronghold into an Urborg volcano and Skyshroud Forest into Keld, and both left scars. The last and worst came over Otaria, when Karona, the goddess who was Dominaria's magic made flesh, was destroyed.",
                        "The rifts fed on one another and grew. Through them came things that did not belong. Creatures from a thousand years ago walked out of the air. So did creatures from futures that never happened and from histories that had gone another way, and people who stood too near were sometimes pulled out of their own time entirely. Mages learned to draw power through the tears. Planeswalkers who stepped near one were struck down by pain and visions of other realities. {Jhoira of the Ghitu}, the artificer who had sailed with the Weatherlight, appears as she was in this age, older and harder, still working.",
                        "The worst of it was invisible to the people living through it. Dominaria was the nexus of the Multiverse, the one plane every other plane leaned on. If it broke, it would not break alone. And Shiv, phased out of time three hundred years before, was about to come back to a world that no longer had a place for it to fit.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-talon-gates", "The Talon Gates",
                    listOf(
                        "[Teferi](who/teferi) came home with Jhoira to bring back the lands he had hidden, and found the world dying. Freyalise, still guarding her elves in Skyshroud, would not hear of anything beyond her forest. So Teferi gathered a smaller company. Radha was a Keldon half-elf warlord, fierce and young. Venser was a scavenger from the swamps of Urborg who had built a teleportation throne he called the Ambulator out of salvaged Phyrexian parts. Both of them carried sparks that had not yet ignited, and the rifts had bent both sparks into something strange.",
                        "A rift caught them in Urborg and flung them, half lost, onto the beach at Madara beside the Talon Gates, the bones of the ancient leviathan. Something there had been waiting. [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had [died centuries before](elder-dragons/god-emperor-of-madara), but a remnant of him lingered, tied to the rift his old battle had made, and it spoke to them as Sensei Ryu. It took hold of Venser's mind and used his spark as a ladder. The dragon climbed back into the world in a body pulled through the rift from his own past.",
                        "Teferi returned in time to challenge him. It was hardly a fight. Bolas walked through Teferi's spells and tore him apart, and was holding his head in one claw when the dying planeswalker told him what the rifts meant. If they were not closed, every plane would fall, Bolas with them. Bolas let him heal. Then he left, promising a terrible vengeance on the family of Tetsuo Umezawa, the man who had killed him the first time, and on anyone who had ever helped them.",
                        "{Radha, Heir to Keld} and {Venser, Shaper Savant} show the two young people Teferi had dragged into this. Neither of them had yet seen the worst of it. The rifts did not only open onto the past. Some of them opened onto other Dominarias.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "other-dominarias", "Other Dominarias",
                    listOf(
                        "Back in Urborg, Venser rebuilt his throne out of Thran metal ringed with new powerstones, and when they tried to use it, the rift at the Stronghold took them somewhere else. They stood in an Urborg where the Ice Age had never ended and Lord Windgrace was fighting a second Phyrexian invasion. The way home was too far for the throne. [Teferi](who/teferi) and Jhoira put their magic behind Venser, his spark finally ignited, and he planeswalked all three of them back to the world they knew.",
                        "Other rifts were leaking those other worlds into this one. Over Skyshroud, Phyrexians poured through from a reality where the invasion had won. Over Urborg came Phyrexians from the endless winter. Windgrace, who hated every artificer alive, put his mark on Venser so he could control him whenever he chose. The mark was removed by the Weaver King, a sorcerer who had quietly made himself master of the slivers of Urborg, and who wanted Venser's mind for his own reasons.",
                        "The Weaver King did not serve himself. He served Leshrac, the nightwalker of the Ice Age, freed long ago from his prison on Phyrexia and back on Dominaria after three centuries out of sight. And Leshrac had a patron of his own. The Myojin of Night's Reach, a great spirit of Kamigawa who wanted [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) stopped, came to him with a gift and asked for nothing, trusting his ambition to do her work. The gift was her mask. With it Leshrac meant to kill Bolas and take his power.",
                        "Venser killed the Weaver King, in the end, with his own planeswalking and the pair of powerstones he had salvaged as a boy. But Leshrac had already found a better tool. A planeswalker named Jeska had arrived on Dominaria looking for her old teacher [Karn](who/karn). Leshrac offered her the mask, and she refused it. He took hold of her mind anyway. The hunt to close the rifts was about to become a race, and the [Mending](the-mending) would be paid for in lives.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Time Spiral, Planar Chaos, Future Sight"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Time Spiral, by Scott McGough and Timothy Sanders"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Planar Chaos, by Scott McGough and Timothy Sanders"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Time rift, Time Spiral crisis, Venser, Leshrac, Nicol Bolas"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "the-mending", "The Mending",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "shiv-and-skyshroud", "Shiv and Skyshroud",
                    listOf(
                        "There was only one way anyone knew to close a rift. A planeswalker had to pour themselves into it. [Teferi](who/teferi) went first, at Shiv, because Shiv was his doing. He gave up his spark to seal the tear, and the volcanic land he had hidden three hundred years before came back into the world whole, with Jhoira's people still on it. Teferi was mortal afterward. He could still work magic, but he would never walk between worlds again, or so everyone believed.",
                        "Freyalise had refused to help anyone outside her forest. Then the Phyrexians of another reality came through the rift above Skyshroud, and the Weaver King took her slivers out from under her command, and she fought until she had nothing left. At the end, exhausted, she understood what Teferi had done and why. She called back all the power she had spent for three centuries keeping her forest warm in the cold of Keld, drew it into herself, and turned it on the rift.",
                        "The blast of green and red mana sealed the tear. It also killed her, and with her the forest she had sheltered since the Rathi Overlay. Of all Skyshroud a single tree was left standing. {Freyalise, Llanowar's Fury} remembers the goddess as the elves of Llanowar did, armoured and furious. After more than a thousand years of godhood she died the way the mortals she had looked down on usually did, for a place she loved.",
                        "Two rifts were closed and two planeswalkers spent. Lord Windgrace in Urborg had heard of Freyalise's death and grieved for her. He was the next to understand what it would take.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "urborg-and-tolaria", "Urborg and Tolaria",
                    listOf(
                        "Lord Windgrace had guarded Urborg for longer than anyone could remember, and the rift over the Stronghold was pouring Phyrexians into it. Before he faced the rift he cast one last spell, fusing his spirit to the land so that something of him would always watch over the swamps. Then he turned his magic on the tear. As it folded shut it drew the life out of him, and when it was gone, so was he. {Lord Windgrace} shows the panther king. His followers still haunt Urborg, sabotaging the Cabal's machines.",
                        "Jhoira called for help from the one being she trusted most. [Karn](who/karn), the silver golem who had been built to travel in time and who had inherited [Urza](who/urza)'s spark at the end of the [Apocalypse](apocalypse), answered. Venser met him in the Blind Eternities and went with him toward Tolaria, where the rift was the worst of all, because the archmage Barrin had obliterated the whole island during the invasion and left nothing to mend.",
                        "So Karn went back. He travelled to the days of the invasion, to the moment just before Barrin's spell, and sealed the rift there, and the sealing took his spark. He was flung back into the present changed. Inside him, from a heartstone a Phyrexian had given him long ago, a trace of glistening oil had been waiting, and with the spark gone it began to stir. Venser was the last person Karn spoke to. He left a message that no one should follow him, and fled to Mirrodin, the plane he had made, where the oil would do its work in [Scars](scars).",
                        "Venser carried the news back with a heavy heart, and found a stranger waiting. She was Jeska, Karn's apprentice, and she had already decided that Teferi's way of closing rifts, which cost a planeswalker every time, was no way at all.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "jeskas-lens", "Jeska's Lens",
                    listOf(
                        "Jeska had lived three lives. She had been a barbarian girl of Otaria, then Phage, whose touch rotted anything living, then one third of the goddess Karona. When Karona died, Jeska's spark ignited and saved her, and [Karn](who/karn) had taught her to walk the planes. Now Leshrac's hand was on her mind, and she did not know it. She had a plan that seemed brilliant. She did not need to sacrifice herself. She could use someone else's damaged spark as a lens.",
                        "She took Radha in Keld and carried her to Zhalfir. There, without a word to [Teferi](who/teferi), she forced Radha's power through the rift and slammed it shut. Zhalfir had been waiting three hundred years to come home. Sealed at the wrong moment, it could not. The phased lands were set adrift in the Multiverse as a pocket plane, and Teferi, mortal now, could only say that he might have saved them if he still had his spark.",
                        "Then she took Radha to Yavimaya. The forest's spirit Multani reached into her mind to calm her. Leshrac answered with visions of her brother Kamahl killing his own allies, and Jeska burned Multani's body to ash and used his power and Radha's together to close the rift. Radha lived. Her spark did not, and with it went the planeswalker she might have become. {Jeska, Thrice Reborn} shows the woman who did all of this, half free and half a puppet.",
                        "Two more rifts were closed and two more terrible prices paid. The next rift was at Madara, beside the bones of the leviathan, and that was where Leshrac had wanted her all along.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "madara", "Madara",
                    listOf(
                        "At the Talon Gates Leshrac sprang his trap. With the mask of Night's Reach he tore Phage's old killing power out of Jeska and took it for himself, and when [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) came, Leshrac challenged him to a duel. He meant Jeska to fight beside him. She refused, with some quiet help from the dragon, and Leshrac sealed her in stone so she would not interfere.",
                        "The duel ranged across worlds. Leshrac caught Bolas between the Talon Gates and pinned him there, cutting him off from planeswalking, and began to rot the dragon's body away with Phage's touch. They fought from plane to plane, and wherever they passed pieces of dragon fell, on Ravnica, on Kamigawa, on Mercadia, on Ulgrotha. When they came back to Dominaria Leshrac began his killing spell, sure that he had won.",
                        "He had not. In the last seconds Bolas drove the bones of his own tail through Leshrac and told him the truth. He had already beaten the Myojin of Night's Reach, and he held her true mask, far greater than the copy she had given Leshrac. The dragon regrew his body in an instant and pulled Leshrac into the mask. Minutes later he used the nightwalker's trapped power to close the Madaran rift, and that was the end of Leshrac.",
                        "Bolas freed Jeska and told her, almost kindly, that the last rift could not be closed. The rift over Otaria filled the whole sky, and it had been made by the death of Karona. By the death of Jeska, in other words, or of the goddess she had been part of. It belonged to her.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "otaria", "Otaria",
                    listOf(
                        "Jeska went to Otaria to face it alone. Venser and Radha, who had every reason to hate her, came after her and offered to stand beside her, and she let them, and then in the heart of the rift she threw them both back out of it so that they would not be hurt by a mistake that was hers. She faced an image of Karona at the center of the storm and broke it. Then she spread her own spark across the rift and past it, across every rift and fissure in the Multiverse that touched it, and in one great discharge closed them all.",
                        "She died in it. The tales say that she woke in a white void where her long dead brother Kamahl was waiting for her, and that she was glad. What she began is called the Great Mending. Mana flowed back into Dominaria at once, and the dying world healed with startling speed. Everything the rifts had dragged out of the past went home to its own time. {The Mending of Dominaria} shows the moment the land began to live again.",
                        "Something else changed with it, everywhere, all at once. The spark itself was rewritten. Planeswalkers lost their immortality and their godlike power. They aged now, and could be hurt, and needed to eat. They could no longer carry other people with them between planes, and could take little more than the clothes they wore. Every device ever built to cross between worlds stopped working. Venser had been the first of the new kind, and now every planeswalker was. {Fraying Omnipotence} names what happened to the old ones.",
                        "For most walkers it was a loss of some degree. For a few it was a fall from heaven. [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) had been a god for twenty-five thousand years, and woke after the Mending as a dragon among dragons, with most of his ancient knowledge gone. He would spend the rest of the modern story trying to win it back, beginning on the shattered plane of [Alara](alara).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The Great Mending that healed the Multiverse also unraveled the threads of Nicol Bolas's power.", "Fraying Omnipotence"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Time Spiral, Planar Chaos, Future Sight"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Planar Chaos, by Scott McGough and Timothy Sanders"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Future Sight, by John Delaney"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Great Mending, Time rift, Jeska, Leshrac, Karn, Freyalise, Windgrace"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "gatewatch", "Gatewatch",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "five-origins", "Five Origins",
                    listOf(
                        "The story of the Gatewatch begins with five people on five worlds, each of them broken open. On Theros a young hero of Akros named Kytheon led a band of friends he called his Irregulars, and his pride got them killed. His spark ignited over their bodies, and in time he took a new name, [Gideon](who/gideon). {Kytheon, Hero of Akros} is the boy before the loss. On Vryn a young telepath was trained by a sphinx called Alhammarret, who lied to him about the day his spark first flickered. When [Jace](who/jace) finally forced his way into his master's mind and learned the truth, the fight shattered his own memory, and his spark ignited in the wreck.",
                        "On Dominaria, [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess tried to save her brother Josu with a forbidden cure and turned him into a walking corpse instead. Her spark ignited in that horror, long before the Mending. Afterward, aging and losing power, she signed away her soul to four demons in a contract that [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) brokered for her. On Kaladesh the Consulate burned [Chandra](who/chandra) Nalaar's village to find her. Her father died there. Chandra was led out to be executed, and her spark ignited in the flames and carried her to another world, believing her mother dead as well.",
                        "On Zendikar an elf girl of the Joraga named Nissa Revane heard the soul of her world crying out. It showed her something writhing beneath a mountain, the imprisoned Eldrazi, and the sight of them ignited her spark. {Jace, Vryn's Prodigy}, {Liliana, Heretical Healer}, {Chandra, Fire of Kaladesh} and {Nissa, Vastwood Seer} each flip into a planeswalker, which is the whole of their stories in two faces.",
                        "For years the five went their own ways. Jace and Liliana were lovers and enemies on more than one plane. Chandra trained, and rebelled, among the fire mages of a monastery on Regatha. Gideon made a home on Ravnica. They had no reason to stand together until something came that none of them could face alone.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sea-gate", "Sea Gate",
                    listOf(
                        "What came was the Eldrazi. On Zendikar the titans Ulamog and Kozilek had broken free and were eating the world, and [Gideon](who/gideon) was there leading the defense of Sea Gate, the great lighthouse city on the coast. [Jace](who/jace) arrived looking for answers about the hedrons and found [Ugin](who/ugin), the spirit dragon, who told him how the old prison worked. Jace saw something Ugin had not meant to show him. The same network that had bound the titans might be used to kill them.",
                        "The [Battle for Zendikar](battle-for-zendikar) was very nearly lost. Before the end, Gideon, Jace, [Chandra](who/chandra) and Nissa fought the demon Ob Nixilis together and beat him, and in the ruins of Sea Gate they understood that alone each of them was helpless against threats like this one, and together they might stand against almost anything. One by one they swore an oath to watch over the Multiverse and come when they were needed. They named themselves for the city they had sworn it in. {Oath of Gideon} and the other oath cards record each promise.",
                        "Then they did what Jace had planned. Nissa raised the sunken hedrons into a ring, the titans were bound in place, and Chandra poured a fire through them so vast that Ulamog and Kozilek burned away to nothing. Ugin was furious. The titans were ancient beyond reckoning, and killing them, he warned, would have consequences no one could foresee, and [Sorin](who/sorin) Markov, who had helped bind them long ago, would be less forgiving than he was.",
                        "Jace went to Innistrad to find Sorin and instead found a plane going mad. The third titan, Emrakul, was not on Zendikar at all.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("I've heard it said that a Planeswalker is someone who can always run from danger. But Gideon's right: we're also the ones who can choose to stay.", "Jace Beleren, on Call the Gatewatch"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "moon-and-aether", "Moon and Aether",
                    listOf(
                        "On Innistrad, [Jace](who/jace) and the moonfolk planeswalker Tamiyo found [Emrakul](emrakul-on-innistrad) twisting the whole plane into her shape. He brought the others. [Liliana](who/liliana) came too, uninvited, with an army of zombies and the Chain Veil, an artifact that let her strike the titan directly and nearly killed her doing it. While she lay unconscious, Tamiyo and Nissa sealed Emrakul inside Innistrad's [moon](eldritch-moon). When Liliana woke, healed by the others, she swore the oath herself. She told no one that she meant to use them.",
                        "They made Jace's rooms on Ravnica their home. An envoy from Kaladesh named Dovin Baan came asking them to guard an inventors' fair, and Liliana told him the Gatewatch did not handle local trouble. [Chandra](who/chandra) heard the name of her home and left for it, and Liliana went with her. On Kaladesh Chandra learned that her mother Pia had survived the fire after all and was leading the renegades against the Consulate.",
                        "Then they found out who was behind the Consulate. Tezzeret, the artificer who had served [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) for years, was on Kaladesh, gathering the plane's inventions and inventors for something larger. [Gideon](who/gideon), Jace and Nissa followed, and the Gatewatch joined the renegades in open revolt. They won, and along the way gained a sixth member, Ajani Goldmane, the white leonin who had fought Bolas once before and knew exactly what he was.",
                        "Ajani begged them to wait, to gather allies, to understand the enemy before they faced him. The others would not wait. Tezzeret had fled through a portal to a plane called Amonkhet, and the Gatewatch went after him.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "broken-on-amonkhet", "Broken on Amonkhet",
                    listOf(
                        "On [Amonkhet](amonkhet) they found a whole world raised to die for [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), and an army of undead champions waiting for him. When he came, they fought him together, and he beat each of them in turn. He threw [Gideon](who/gideon) aside with his tail and crushed [Chandra](who/chandra) in his claw. He turned Nissa's bond with the land against her. He broke into [Jace](who/jace)'s mind and nearly destroyed it. He offered [Liliana](who/liliana) a deal, and she took it and left. The rest fled one by one. It was the worst defeat of their lives.",
                        "Most of them found their way to Dominaria, where Ajani had told them to meet. Jace did not. A failsafe [Ugin](who/ugin) had hidden in his mind had wiped his memory and sent him to Ixalan, where he was trapped. Nissa, grieving and angry at Liliana, left the team. Chandra left soon after. Only Gideon and Liliana remained, and they went aboard the new Weatherlight to fight the Cabal and its demon lord Belzenlok, the last of the four demons who held Liliana's contract.",
                        "Chandra came back. So, at last, did Jace. With [Teferi](who/teferi), [Karn](who/karn) and the pyromancer Jaya Ballard they stormed the Cabal's Stronghold and killed Belzenlok, and Gideon came away with the Blackblade, an ancient sword from the days of the elder dragons. Teferi, his spark restored by Jhoira, swore the oath. {Gideon Blackblade} shows Gideon with his new weapon, ready for the dragon.",
                        "But with the last demon dead Liliana's contract did not dissolve. It passed to the dragon who had brokered it. When the others left Dominaria to hunt Bolas, Liliana went to him instead, and the next time the Gatewatch saw her she was leading his army.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "gideons-last-oath", "Gideon's Last Oath",
                    listOf(
                        "The Gatewatch went to Ravnica for the final battle, and found they had walked into the trap Bolas had built for them. The [War of the Spark](bolas-war-of-the-spark) is told from the dragon's side in its own place, and from the city's in [another](ravnica-war-of-the-spark). For the Gatewatch it was the end. [Gideon](who/gideon) led the charge against the God-Eternal Rhonas and killed it with the Blackblade. When he struck at [Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) himself the sword shattered, because the dragon had long since enchanted it never to harm an elder dragon again.",
                        "[Jace](who/jace), Teferi and two others were sent to kill [Liliana](who/liliana), and almost did. Then Liliana watched a girl in the street shield her little brother from a falling wall and die with him, and she turned the dreadhorde on Bolas. He invoked her contract, and she began to come apart. Gideon put himself between her and it. He had the hieromancy to take a bargain onto himself, and he took hers, and it killed him. {Gideon's Sacrifice} is the oath kept to its end.",
                        "Bolas fell. The survivors held a memorial for Gideon on Theros, where he had been born, and then they scattered. A few gathered again when the Phyrexians came. Teferi, [Chandra](who/chandra) and the new member Kaya fought the Eternal Night on Innistrad. Jace gathered the strike team that failed on New Phyrexia. Some of them were compleated, and some came back.",
                        "When the Phyrexians were finally beaten, the sparks of many of the Gatewatch faded. Omenpaths opened between worlds and anyone could cross, and a promise made by people who could walk where no one else could no longer meant what it had. The story of that ending is [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("To Liliana's surprise, the cost of betraying Bolas did not fall on her.", "Gideon's Sacrifice"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Magic Origins, Battle for Zendikar, Oath of the Gatewatch, Eldritch Moon, Kaladesh, Aether Revolt, Amonkhet, Hour of Devastation, Dominaria, War of the Spark"),
                StorySource("Story", "Magic Origins, Oath of the Gatewatch, Amonkhet and Dominaria stories, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Novel", "War of the Spark: Ravnica, by Greg Weisman"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Gatewatch, Gideon Jura, Jace Beleren, Liliana Vess, Nissa Revane"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "sparks-lost", "Sparks Lost",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "compleated-sparks", "Compleated Sparks",
                    listOf(
                        "For as long as there had been Phyrexians, planeswalkers had been safe from one thing. Compleation took the soul, and the spark lived in the soul, so a compleated planeswalker would simply be a Phyrexian without a spark. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) set the praetor Jin-Gitaxias to solve that, and he did. He found a way to compleat a planeswalker while keeping enough of the soul to hold the spark, and Tamiyo of Kamigawa was the first to go under his knives.",
                        "Then the strike team went to New Phyrexia. [Jace](who/jace) had gathered planeswalkers from across the Multiverse to carry a remade Sylex into the Seedcore and destroy Realmbreaker, the world tree Norn was growing through the planes. A planar shield scattered them on arrival. [Nahiri](who/nahiri) was scratched in the first fight and began to turn. Vraska was captured and fought for hours before she fell. Lukka was compleated and sent hunting for Nissa, and Nissa was taken too. When the survivors found Vraska she infected Jace.",
                        "At the core Elspeth Tirel took the Sylex and planeswalked away with it as it went off, and the mission failed. Norn's compleated planeswalkers led the invasion of their own worlds. Nahiri went to Zendikar, Vraska to Ravnica, Tamiyo to Kamigawa, Lukka to Ikoria. Ajani, compleated earlier as a sleeper on Dominaria, went to Theros and turned its gods. The story of the war is told in [Invasion of the Multiverse](invasion-of-the-multiverse). This is the story of what it did to sparks.",
                        "{Nissa, Resurgent Animist} and {Nahiri, Forged in Fury} show two of them after it was over, no longer planeswalkers at all. Neither of them lost her spark in the same way, and neither was the last.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "zhalfir-switched", "Zhalfir Switched",
                    listOf(
                        "The invasion ended because of a tree. Wrenn was a dryad planeswalker bonded to a treefolk called Seven, and she and [Chandra](who/chandra) went back to New Phyrexia to try again. Fighting their way to the Seedcore beside the Mirran rebel Koth, they were joined by Elspeth, returned from the Blind Eternities as an archangel. Wrenn put her hands on Realmbreaker and bonded with it.",
                        "Through its branches she found Zhalfir, the land [Teferi](who/teferi) had phased out of Dominaria centuries before, drifting as a pocket plane. Teferi was there. A failed journey through time had stranded him in Zhalfir, and in a place where time ran strangely he had raised an army. Through Realmbreaker he led it against New Phyrexia, with angels from Capenna carrying Halo, the angelic essence that burned Phyrexians. In that battle Jin-Gitaxias, Vorinclex and [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) died.",
                        "Then Wrenn did the impossible. She used Realmbreaker to swap the places of Zhalfir and New Phyrexia in the Blind Eternities, so that Zhalfir was joined to the Multiverse again and New Phyrexia was cut off from it. The glistening oil on every plane lost its source at once. Some Phyrexians froze where they stood. Some crumbled. The compleated fell down as if asleep. Wrenn did not survive what she had done. Teferi found her ashes at the base of the tree, took a single acorn from them, and planted it on a hill in Zhalfir. {Teferi Akosa of Zhalfir} is the general who led that charge.",
                        "The compleated were asleep, not cured. Some could be brought back. The price of bringing them back was another spark.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "karns-gift", "Karn's Gift",
                    listOf(
                        "[Karn](who/karn) had been a planeswalker twice. The first spark had been [Urza](who/urza)'s, and he had given it up in the [Mending](the-mending) to close the rift at Tolaria. The second had come from Venser, who gave his life and his spark to free Karn from the Phyrexian oil at the heart of New Phyrexia years before. Karn had carried that gift ever since.",
                        "With the healer Melira, whose body had always been immune to the oil, Karn performed a ritual to undo the compleation of Ajani Goldmane and Nissa Revane. Teferi helped. It worked, and it took Venser's spark to do it. Karn walked away from the ritual unable to leave whatever world he stood on, and Ajani and Nissa walked away as themselves. Ajani would keep his spark through everything that followed. Nissa would not.",
                        "[Nahiri](who/nahiri)'s ended stranger still. When she was cured, her spark was left sealed inside a hedron, a stone of the kind she had spent thousands of years carving, and when the hedron shattered the spark was lost with it. Vraska was freed from Norn's control on Ravnica when a memory [Jace](who/jace) had hidden in her mind years earlier woke up in the middle of a fight. Tamiyo and Lukka were not so lucky. Both died compleated, and Wrenn and Tibalt died too.",
                        "Those were sparks spent or broken one at a time, by people who chose it or had it done to them. What came next was not chosen by anyone. In the weeks after the war, planeswalkers on every plane began to notice that something was missing.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-desparkening", "The Desparkening",
                    listOf(
                        "It happened quietly. The spark faded out of planeswalkers across the Multiverse so gradually that many did not notice until they tried to leave and could not. There was no death in it, and their souls stayed whole. Those who lost it described a hole, a missing limb that still hurt. They called it the Desparkening, and wherever each planeswalker stood when the spark went out, they stayed.",
                        "Most of them had gone home to defend their own worlds, so most were stranded on the planes they were born on. [Teferi](who/teferi) lost his. So did [Liliana](who/liliana), who had just held the school of Strixhaven against the invasion. Sarkhan Vol lost his on Tarkir, and with it the dragon form he had lived for, which would turn him toward madness in [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm). Narset, Vraska, Kiora, Koth, Saheeli, Huatli, Ob Nixilis, the Wanderer and many others lost theirs. {Spark Rupture} shows the moment, and {Ob Nixilis, Captive Kingpin} the old demon after it.",
                        "A handful kept their sparks for reasons no one understands. [Chandra](who/chandra) did. So did Ajani, Elspeth, Jace, Kaito, Kaya, Ral Zarek, Tezzeret, Oko, Ashiok and [Ugin](who/ugin), and young Quintorius Kand, whose spark had ignited during the invasion itself. No one knows why. Teferi thought the Multiverse might be healing itself from the Sylex blast and the violence of Wrenn's switch. Saheeli believed the sparks had been moved rather than destroyed, since mana and aether can be neither made nor unmade.",
                        "And in the same days, [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) opened across the Multiverse, doors between planes left behind where Realmbreaker's branches had torn through. For the first time since the Mending, anyone could walk to another world. The researcher Zimone Wola argued that this was the cause, and that once the planes were joined by doors the Multiverse no longer needed planeswalkers to hold it together. Whatever the reason, the age of the planeswalker was over, and the [Omenpath Era](the-omenpaths) had begun.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Phyrexia was defeated, but the repercussions of the invasion rippled through the Blind Eternities.", "Spark Rupture"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One, March of the Machine, March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine and March of the Machine: The Aftermath, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Desparkening, New Phyrexia's Invasion of the Multiverse, Karn, Teferi, Gatewatch"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
